---
import TreatmentPage from "../../../components/TreatmentPage.astro";

const T = "/services/fertility-treatment";
const A = "/services/assessment";

const page = {
  slug: "ovulation-test",
  name: "Ovulation Assessment",
  title: "Ovulation Test & Cycle Assessment | Kamran Fertility Clinic",
  description: "Not sure if you are ovulating? Dr. Kamran Saleem reviews your cycle and advises on ovulation kits, progesterone blood tests and follicle ultrasound.",
  eyebrow: "Ovulation Assessment",
  h1: "Ovulation Test and Cycle Assessment",
  waText: "Hello, I would like to book an ovulation assessment with Dr. Kamran Saleem.",
  ctaLabel: "Book an assessment",
  heroSub: [
    "An ovulation assessment helps clarify when you ovulate, whether you ovulate regularly, and whether further testing may be useful.",
    "The consultation reviews your menstrual pattern and discusses ovulation tests, blood tests and ultrasound monitoring that may fit your situation.",
  ].join(" "),
  leadIn: [
    { rich: ["Ovulation testing is one part of a wider fertility evaluation. The tests selected depend on your history, symptoms and previous results. Explore the related ", ["Fertility Assessment & Diagnosis", A], " pathway, or read the educational guide ", ["Ovulation Test: How to Track Your Cycle", "/resources/ovulation-test"], "."] },
  ],
  sections: [
    {
      id: "ovulation-assessment-purpose",
      title: "What Ovulation Assessment Can Help Clarify",
      intro: "The assessment considers your cycle history and whether a test is needed to investigate ovulation.",
      icon: "calendar",
      body: [
        "Ovulation is the release of an egg from an ovary, usually once in each cycle. Pregnancy is most likely when intercourse takes place in the days leading up to and including ovulation, because sperm can survive in the reproductive tract for several days while the egg survives for a much shorter time.",
        "Cycle patterns can offer clues, but irregular cycles or uncertainty about timing may need further assessment. A clinician can discuss whether home ovulation predictor kits, selected blood tests or ultrasound monitoring are appropriate for your situation.",
      ],
    },
    {
      id: "signs-ovulation-may-be-irregular",
      title: "Signs Ovulation May Be Irregular or Absent",
      intro: "Some patterns are worth discussing, although none of them confirms a problem on its own.",
      icon: "clipboard",
      body: [
        { ul: [
          "Periods that are irregular, very frequent, infrequent or hard to predict",
          "Cycles that are much shorter or longer than usual for you",
          "No period for several months without pregnancy",
          "No clear LH surge on home ovulation kits over several cycles",
          "A history of polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS), thyroid problems or raised prolactin",
          "Trying to conceive for a year or more (or six months if you are 35 or older)",
        ] },
        "Regular periods usually, but not always, mean ovulation is happening. Irregular periods do not necessarily mean you never ovulate. That is why a clinician reviews the whole picture before suggesting tests.",
      ],
    },
    {
      id: "methods-of-assessment",
      title: "Ovulation Testing Methods Compared",
      intro: "Different methods answer different questions and are not interchangeable in every situation.",
      icon: "compare",
      body: [
        { ul: [
          "Cycle tracking and symptoms: recording period dates, cervical mucus changes and, if you wish, basal body temperature can show a pattern over several months",
          "Home urine LH kits (ovulation predictor kits) detect the hormone surge that often comes before ovulation, helping you time intercourse",
          "Progesterone blood testing, timed to the cycle, may be used to assess whether ovulation likely occurred",
          "Ultrasound follicle monitoring may be recommended in selected situations to follow follicle growth and confirm that a follicle matures and releases an egg",
        ] },
        "A positive LH kit shows a hormone surge, not proof that an egg was released. This is one reason blood tests or ultrasound may be added when a firm answer is needed.",
      ],
    },
    {
      id: "using-ovulation-kits",
      title: "Using Ovulation Predictor Kits Well",
      intro: "Kits are widely available and can be useful when used with the right timing.",
      icon: "flask",
      body: [
        { ul: [
          "Read the instructions for your brand, as testing schedules and sensitivity vary",
          "Work out your starting day from your usual cycle length, not a fixed calendar date",
          "Test at a similar time each day and avoid drinking large amounts of fluid beforehand",
          "Ovulation commonly follows a positive result within about a day or two, so intercourse around this time is generally advised",
          "Keep a record of results and share it at your consultation",
        ] },
        "Women with PCOS or very irregular cycles may see several apparent surges or none at all, so kit results can be harder to interpret. If you are testing for several cycles without a clear pattern, ask a clinician for guidance rather than repeating the same approach.",
      ],
    },
    {
      id: "causes-of-irregular-ovulation",
      title: "Possible Reasons Ovulation May Be Affected",
      intro: "Ovulation depends on several hormones working together, so there is more than one possible explanation.",
      icon: "flask",
      body: [
        { ul: [
          "Polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS)",
          "Thyroid or prolactin hormone changes",
          "Significant weight change, intense exercise or prolonged stress",
          "Reduced ovarian reserve or approaching menopause",
          "Certain medicines or recent hormonal contraception",
        ] },
        { rich: ["Hormone tests may help investigate these questions; see our ", ["hormonal analysis", `${A}/hormonal-analysis`], " page, or read ", ["PCOS and Fertility", "/resources/pcos-and-fertility"], " for background."] },
      ],
    },
    {
      id: "planning-next-steps",
      title: "Planning Around Your Cycle",
      intro: "The right timing depends on cycle length and the reason for testing.",
      icon: "route",
      body: [
        "Before your appointment, note the first day of your last several periods, any home test results, medicines and supplements, and any previous scans or reports. This helps the clinician advise on timing and whether another assessment is needed.",
        { rich: ["For related assessment, see ", ["hormonal analysis", `${A}/hormonal-analysis`], " and ", ["female fertility assessment", `${A}/female-fertility-test`], ". If a partner’s evaluation is also needed, see ", ["male fertility testing", `${A}/male-fertility-test`], "."] },
        { rich: ["If an ovulation problem is identified, treatment options can be discussed in the context of your wider evaluation. You can read about the ", ["fertility treatment", T], " services we offer."] },
      ],
    },
  ],
  faqs: [
    { q: "Can a home ovulation kit confirm that I ovulated?", a: "A urine LH kit detects a hormone surge that may precede ovulation; it does not directly confirm that an egg was released. Progesterone testing or ultrasound can be used when confirmation is needed." },
    { q: "When should I take an ovulation test?", a: "Timing depends on your usual cycle length and the kit instructions. If cycles are irregular, ask a clinician how to plan testing." },
    { q: "Do I need an ultrasound to check ovulation?", a: "Not everyone does. Ultrasound monitoring may be considered when it answers a specific clinical question, such as irregular cycles or repeated unclear kit results." },
    { q: "Can I ovulate and still have irregular periods?", a: "Yes. Some women ovulate irregularly or late in the cycle. A clinician can help work out whether ovulation is occurring and when." },
    { q: "Can I ovulate without having a period?", a: "It is possible to ovulate before a first period or before periods return after pregnancy, but absent periods usually deserve medical review." },
    { q: "When should I see a doctor about ovulation?", a: "Consider an assessment if your cycles are irregular or absent, or if you have been trying to conceive for 12 months (6 months if you are 35 or older). Seek advice sooner if you already know of a relevant condition." },
  ],
  closingHeading: "Ovulation Assessment with Dr. Kamran Saleem",
  closingParas: [
    "Dr. Kamran Saleem can review your cycle history and available reports, discuss whether an ovulation assessment is appropriate, and explain possible next steps. The exact tests and plan depend on your individual circumstances.",
    "You can ask about the consultation and any separate test fees before confirming an appointment.",
  ],
  resources: [
    { href: "/resources/ovulation-test", category: "Fertility Assessment", title: "Ovulation Test: How to Track Your Cycle" },
    { href: "/resources/pcos-and-fertility", category: "Fertility Assessment", title: "PCOS and Fertility" },
    { href: "/resources/hormone-test--for-fertility", category: "Fertility Assessment", title: "Hormone Test for Fertility" },
  ],
  close: {
    heading: "Need Help Choosing the Right Assessment?",
    body: "Share your cycle dates, symptoms or previous reports when booking so the consultation can focus on your questions.",
    cta: "Book an assessment",
  },
};
---

<TreatmentPage {...page} />
